let me start by saying right from the  get-go how much I loved this movie you  did a phenomenal job one of my favorites  of the year thank you um I loved the way  you shot this film you and Robbie  collaborated just the the fish I the  fish lens the choice of lenses the  everything I loved it talked a little  bit but collaborating with Robbie on the  look at the film for my last film few  films I think I've been experimenting  with certain things movement lensing  camera angles and things like that so I  felt that this film was an opportunity  to take things even further and also a  good opportunity to work with Robbie  that I had met a few times and we were  always discussing about working together  at some point I really loved his work so  yeah there were some initial ideas that  I had coming into the film I really  wanted to enhance the feel of those  extremely big spaces and how they how  they're juxtaposed to the few people  that inhabit them so the use of you know  extreme wide-angle lenses kind of  enhanced that feel and it created those  huge distorted spaces and it also at the  same time made the film feel quite  claustrophobic because you kind of see  where the space ends and it's not  infinite although it's big we discussed  a lot about camera movement and we  wanted to have within the same shot more  than one of the characters so there was  a lot of movement and panning and  [Music]  another thing is that I always like to  work with natural light or practical  lighting so in in our case you know  there was no electricity during that  period so we had to work with either  daylight or candles which was exciting  and kind of scary at the same time but  yeah Robby's really up for trying  anything and going anyplace you direct  him to and it was a great experience we  started a show on collider called for  your consideration  and one of the things we've been talking  about with everyone recently is what  does it mean to be an Oscar movie  especially with the 